text:
Alex: Quick heads-up — saw a production perf regression after the recent kernel-selection tweak. Frontend started getting spikes of 429s for small/medium requests on pool-12. I think admission needs hysteresis instead of our current hard-threshold reject.

Jordan: What did the profiler show? Is this kernel-switch cost or batching backlog?

Alex: Profiler points to kernel warmup + slot swapping when sequences flip between quantized int8 and fp16 paths. When batch formation toggles, the runtime oscillates and we see batch-delay spikes.

Maya: Also noticing KV cache miss-rate rising when the batcher favors many small batches. Cold KV starts correlate with the same windows.

Sam: Proposal: introduce a token_budget target per batch and a soft/hard cap with hysteresis. e.g. target=256, soft_cap=384, hard_cap=512. Admit new requests if current_tokens + req_tokens <= soft_cap, else queue with backoff; only reject when > hard_cap. That should reduce kernel toggles.

Priya: +1. And we should implement a ramped 429 strategy: 1) soft-shed low-priority requests (drop or delay), 2) emit a backpressure signal with Retry-After (429) when queue > soft_cap for a sustained window, 3) hard reject beyond hard_cap. Need per-priority counters and telemetry. :thinking:

Alex: Can someone sketch the pseudo-change to the admission control? I can wire the config and rollout if we agree.

Jordan: Sketch below — very rough.

```
// admission: token-based with hysteresis
int target = cfg.target_tokens; // 256
int soft = cfg.soft_tokens;   // 384
int hard = cfg.hard_tokens;   // 512
if (current_token_load + req_tokens <= soft) {
  admit(req);
} else if (current_token_load + req_tokens <= hard) {
  // queue with priority/backoff, mark as soft-shed candidate
  queue(req);
  emit_metric("admission.soft_shed", 1);
} else {
  reject_with_429(req, RetryAfter);
  emit_metric("admission.hard_reject", 1);
}
```

Maya: I will run targeted microbenchmarks for kernel-switch frequency and kv_miss_rate with these token budgets. Will collect batch_delay_p50/p95 and kernel_switch_count. Posting results in ~2 hours.

Sam: Also add a small hysteresis timer: once we switch kernels, don't allow another kernel-change-trigger for X ms unless queue grows beyond hard. Prevents rapid oscillation.

Priya: Ops checklist: 1) add metrics: kernel_switch_count, batch_delay_p95, kv_miss_rate, admission.soft_shed, admission.hard_reject. 2) Add a canary rollout plan (10% pool, 5m hold). 3) Client-facing 429 format: include Retry-After + reason token_room.

CI-Bot: Linked issue INV-3219 created by Alex: "admission hysteresis + soft 429 ramp to reduce kernel switching regressions" — https://redwood.internal/issue/INV-3219

Alex: Great. I'll open the PR with config toggles (feature flag on by default=false) and the instrumentation hooks. Assigning to @Maya for perf runs and @Sam for rollout.

Maya: Thanks — I'll attach traces and the profiler flamegraphs. If we still see kernel warmups, we might bias batching to prefer homogeneous model variants (prefer same kernel in batch).

Sam: If tests look good, we can merge to canary today and monitor. If p95 improves, roll further.

Priya: :thumbsup: please make sure the client 429 has a stable schema so SDKs can act on Retry-After + token_room reason.

Alex: Filed notes and reproducible steps in the issue. Keeping coordination here.
